# SC4-Decomp architectural rules (summary of AGENT_RULES.md / NEXT_AGENT.md)
- Hand-write clean C++20 per decompiled function; unique descriptive names recorded in notes/ledger.csv, zero duplicates.
- Replace SL::sp* globals with small host/seam interfaces; keep original quirks and comment them.
- One test per class in scripts/tests + entry in scripts/run_tests.sh; build with ASan/UBSan.
- Commit locally after each class, never push; end commits with the Co-Authored-By line.
- Never commit game binaries, assets or Ghidra DBs; the Steam folder is read-only.
- Rendering target is native Metal (no OpenGL); audio must be bug-free; skip DX7 emulation.
- Ask before sudo, downloads over 500 MB, or touching anything outside SC4-Decomp / Homebrew.

# Engine architecture & optimization rules (from the user)
1. Data-oriented design (ECS) is mandatory for simulation data (vehicles, citizens, buildings, grid nodes): no deep
   inheritance trees, arrays of pointers or scattered heap allocations; pack data in contiguous arrays.
2. Optimize CPU-bound loops (pathfinding, A*, water/power propagation, economic ticks) with SIMD intrinsics or
   native vector extensions, never raw ARM/x86 assembly.
3. Cross-platform parity: ARM64 (NEON) and x86_64 (SSE/AVX2) with compile-time switches and a scalar fallback.
4. Profile first: no SIMD for UI, modding layers or one-off init; write clean ECS logic, then vectorize bottlenecks.

# How these rules apply to the recovery work
- src/ classes mirror the original binary one function at a time so they can be checked against Ghidra; they are
  the reference implementation and stay faithful (quirks included).
- The shipping simulation lives in a separate ECS layer (src/ecs/, to be built) that is validated against the
  faithful classes; hot loops there are vectorized per rule 2-4 with a scalar fallback checked bit-for-bit.
